<section class="pagehead">
  <p class="label">About</p>
  <h1 class="pagehead__title">One person,<br>one camera,<br>no sponsors.</h1>
</section>

<section class="prose-page">
  <div class="prose" data-reveal>
    <p>
      This is a travel journal built around photographs rather than itineraries.
      I go somewhere, I shoot it, I keep the handful of frames that worked, and
      I write down what I honestly thought of the place. Nobody pays for a
      mention and there are no affiliate links, which is why some of the
      verdicts are lukewarm.
    </p>

    <h2>The kit</h2>
    <p>
      A Canon EOS R7 with the RF-S 18-150mm on it almost permanently. It is not
      a fast lens and it shows in anything shot after dark, but it means one
      body and no bag. Everything on the site is handheld. Exposure details sit
      under each frame because they explain most of what went right and all of
      what went wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>How the scoring works</h2>
    <p>
      Five points, and the number answers one question only: would I tell
      someone else to go? It is not a rating of how photogenic somewhere is.
      A place can be spectacular and still score three because it is
      overcrowded, overpriced, or better a hundred miles further on.
    </p>

    <h2>Placeholders</h2>
    <p>
      Entries tagged <strong>Placeholder</strong> have not happened yet in this
      form. Their pictures are drawn rather than photographed and their words
      are filler, and they exist so the site has a shape while the real frames
      are still on a card somewhere. They come down as the real entries go up.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
